C MP+race
"JavaScript MM (JSMM) vs C11 -- the separation is NOT thin-air (JSMM, being
 C11-based, ALSO admits OOTA). It is about data races. This is the paper's Fig. 6
 shape: a program that races a non-atomic (JS 'Unordered') access against
 SeqCst atomics on the same location. C11 treats the non-atomic race as
 UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR (catch-fire: any outcome 'allowed' vacuously). JSMM gives
 the racy access a DEFINED, byte-justified semantics and its SC-atomics validity
 rule pins the access in the total order, FORBIDDING the witness. So C11
 'allows' (via catch-fire) an outcome JSMM rules out. herd7's -c11 does not
 implement catch-fire (it models the access as a plain write), so it CANNOT
 adjudicate this distinction -- both verdicts are CITED. See README; the reverse
 direction (mixed-size tearing) is described there too."
{ int b=0; }
P0 (atomic_int* b) {
  atomic_store_explicit(b,1,memory_order_seq_cst);
  int r1 = atomic_load_explicit(b,memory_order_seq_cst);
  *((int*) b) = 2;                                   /* non-atomic (JS Unordered) racy write */
  int r2 = atomic_load_explicit(b,memory_order_seq_cst);
}
P1 (atomic_int* b) {
  atomic_store_explicit(b,1,memory_order_seq_cst);
  atomic_store_explicit(b,2,memory_order_seq_cst);
}
exists (0:r1=1 /\ 0:r2=1)
